    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d168">

                <pb facs="d167-2" n="2" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76ve10/pgd167-2"/>

                <head>168. Telegram 563 From the Embassy in Costa Rica to the Department of
                        State<anchor n="1" xml:id="d168-f1"/>
                    <anchor n="2" xml:id="d168-f2"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>San Jose</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1972-02-27T21:34:00+00:00">February 27, 1969,
                            2134Z</date>
                    </dateline></opener>
                <pb facs="d168-1" n="1" xml:id="pgd168-1"/>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>Subject:</label>
                    <item>Soviet Offer of US Assistance to GOCR</item>
                </list>
                <p>Planning Minister Rodriguez told USAID Director today that Soviet Government has
                    within last few days offered to build/rehabilitate Limon and Puntarenas ports.
                    Projects would be financed in form of loan repayable by Costa Rican coffee
                    exports to USSR, with coffee priced at traditional market levels. Rodriguez
                    implied that proposal was under study by GOCR.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Boonstra</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d168-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d168-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, Nixon Presidential Materials, NSC Files, Box 779,
                    Country Files, Latin America, Costa Rica. Confidential.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d168-f2" n="2" xml:id="d168-f2-content">
                <p>The Embassy reported that the Government of Costa Rican had received an offer
                    from the Soviet Union to rehabilitate two major ports, financed through loans
                    repayable with coffee exports to the Soviet Union.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
